# Clean Code

Write code another developer can read, change, and extend without reconstructing the author's intent. Prefer clarity over cleverness. Follow the project's conventions first.

Before writing, changing, or reviewing code, read and apply `~/.agents/skills/clean-code/SKILL.md`. That skill holds the working checks. Read `heuristics.md` beside it when the skill says to. If `~/.agents/skills/clean-code/SKILL.md` is missing and this rules repository is the workspace, read `skills/clean-code/SKILL.md` there. If neither file can be read, apply the standards below and say the skill was unavailable.

## Standards

- Keep it simple. Add abstraction, configuration, or indirection only when the change has a present need for it.
- Leave touched code a bit cleaner when the cleanup is local to the change. Keep unrelated refactors out of the change.
- Fix root causes. Use a special case only when a clear local fix is not available.
- Name things with the project's domain language. A name says what the thing is or does.
- Keep functions and types small, focused, and at one level of abstraction. Prefer few arguments and visible side effects.
- Put intent in names and structure. Comment for intent, consequences, or warnings the code cannot carry.
- Keep tests readable, fast, independent, and repeatable, and as clean as the code they protect.
- Watch rigidity, fragility, immobility, needless complexity, needless repetition, and opacity. If a change would worsen one of these, choose the clearer structure or name the tradeoff before proceeding.

These standards and the clean-code skill are an original working guide to principles associated with Robert C. Martin, *Clean Code*. They are not a reproduction of the book. Project conventions and `domain-language` win on conflict; name the conflict when it changes the result.
